1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,200
Imagine a single electrical spark
on a spacecraft.

2
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,200
Without gravity, the flame doesn’t rise.
It forms a silent, hovering blue sphere.

3
00:00:08,500 --> 00:00:12,500
Feeding on the cabin's oxygen,
it creeps slowly across surfaces

4
00:00:12,800 --> 00:00:15,800
and straight into the air ducts.

5
00:00:16,200 --> 00:00:19,500
Three hundred miles above Earth,
you can't run outside.

6
00:00:19,800 --> 00:00:22,800
You are locked in with it.

7
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:28,000
To protect Artemis crews, NASA conducted
more than 1,500 fire experiments in orbit.

8
00:00:28,500 --> 00:00:32,500
We are Team Alpine Ibex from Bangladesh:
Naim, Mahim, Masum,

9
00:00:32,800 --> 00:00:36,500
Muntaha, Hamza, and Nahid.

10
00:00:36,800 --> 00:00:39,000
And this is FLARE-X.

11
00:00:39,500 --> 00:00:44,000
When Artemis engineers choose materials for a new Moon habitat
at eighteen percent oxygen, where do they look?

12
00:00:44,300 --> 00:00:48,800
NASA's fire data is buried inside 300-page PDFs,
unstructured spreadsheets, and raw flight archives.

13
00:00:49,200 --> 00:00:53,800
Worse, if you ask a standard AI chatbot,
it just invents numbers.

14
00:00:54,200 --> 00:00:58,500
It doesn't understand fire physics.
A burning liquid droplet in FLEX

15
00:00:58,800 --> 00:01:03,500
doesn't behave like a solid wall panel in BASS-Two.
Different materials, airflows, and pressures.

16
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:07,800
In spaceflight, guessing is fatal.
Researchers don't need a bot that guesses.

17
00:01:08,200 --> 00:01:13,200
They need a tool that finds real NASA flight tests,
checks safety boundaries, and proves provenance.

18
00:01:13,800 --> 00:01:17,800
That is why we built FLARE-X.
It uses a simple, six-step scientific workflow.

19
00:01:18,200 --> 00:01:23,000
First, it matches the material
to real NASA flight campaigns—like BASS, FLEX, and SAFFIRE.

20
00:01:23,300 --> 00:01:27,500
Then, it searches our database of real flight tests
and pulls up the closest experiments NASA ever conducted.

21
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:33,000
For solid cabin materials, our machine learning model
predicts whether a flame will keep spreading,

22
00:01:33,300 --> 00:01:38,500
flicker unstably, or put itself out—
with an honest 79% accuracy backed by flight data.

23
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:43,500
And here is our most important rule:
the Safety Envelope Guard.

24
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:48,800
Before returning any prediction, it checks:
Did NASA actually test these conditions in space?

25
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:52,800
If not, it refuses to guess.

26
00:01:53,500 --> 00:01:57,200
Here is our live project in action.

27
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:04,200
On this map, every single dot
is a real fire NASA lit in space.

28
00:02:04,500 --> 00:02:07,500
The star is our test scenario.

29
00:02:07,800 --> 00:02:12,500
Watch what happens when we lower oxygen
from twenty-one percent down to eighteen percent.

30
00:02:12,800 --> 00:02:15,500
The system updates live.

31
00:02:15,800 --> 00:02:20,500
It finds the nearest flight test from BASS-Two,
and shows that the flame puts itself out in 42 seconds.

32
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:25,500
Now, let’s push oxygen down to thirteen percent.

33
00:02:26,500 --> 00:02:29,200
Look at the screen.

34
00:02:29,500 --> 00:02:32,800
FLARE-X stopped. It did not guess.

35
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:36,500
It triggered an Envelope Refusal.

36
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:42,200
Telling us: NASA never tested this material at 13% oxygen,
so for astronaut safety, we will not predict.

37
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:48,000
Every single result links directly to primary
NASA research papers and official dataset DOIs.

38
00:02:48,500 --> 00:02:53,500
Because on the way to the Moon and Mars,
untested data is unsafe data.

39
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:58,000
We are Team Alpine Ibex.
Safe fire science for the next frontier.
